STEP 1 -- BASELINE, NO SECONDARY ADDRESS.

  awplus# show running-config interface vlan10
  interface vlan10
   ip address 192.168.10.1/24                        <- primary only
  awplus# show ip route
  C       192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10
  C       192.168.48.0/24 is directly connected, vlan48
  (no 172.16.10.0/24 anywhere)

  1a. ARP, feature OFF.  --tag L1a-arp-off, sender 192.168.10.5 / 00:11:22:33:44:01, count 4
      x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.16.10.1  tell 192.168.10.5      -> **0 replies**
      x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 192.168.10.5
      x4  ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43       -> 4 replies, instrument alive
      x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.17.10.1  tell 192.168.10.5      -> **0 replies**, control holds

  1b. 🔎 **A DISCRIMINATOR THAT TURNED OUT NOT TO BE ONE -- recorded because it would have
      produced a false PASS.**  My first design tested "the secondary serves traffic" by sending
      a frame SOURCED in the secondary subnet (172.16.10.5 -> 192.168.48.9, eth3 -> eth4) and
      counting it at eth4.  Run as the baseline with the secondary NOT configured
      (--tag L1b-route-off):
        cap L1b-route-off-eth4.pcap:
          x5  ICMP t8/c0 172.16.10.5 -> 192.168.48.9  [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:09]
      **All five frames crossed anyway.**  Of course they did: IP forwarding is DESTINATION
      based, the destination 192.168.48.9 is a connected subnet, and there is no uRPF here -- the
      source address is not consulted at all.  A leg that gives the same answer with the feature
      on and off measures nothing.  **The baseline-OFF rule caught this before it became a
      verdict.**  The test was redesigned around the direction that does discriminate (1c), and
      the discarded leg is kept here as evidence of what the device does NOT check.

  1c. THE REAL DISCRIMINATOR, feature OFF: traffic from elsewhere INTO the secondary subnet.
      --tag L1c-to-secondary-off, eth4 -> watch eth3, sender 192.168.48.9 / 00:11:22:33:44:09
      cap on eth4 (the sending side):
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 172.16.10.5    [ours]
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 192.168.10.9   [ours]
        x2  ICMP t3/c0 **192.168.48.1** -> 192.168.48.9   <- the DUT's own unreachable
      cap on eth3 (the far side):
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> **192.168.10.9**  [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:0a]
        x1  ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.10.9 tell 192.168.10.1 / x1 ARP-REPLY
      172.16.10.5  : 5 sent, **0 arrived on eth3** -- dropped, with the DUT reporting network
                     unreachable from its ingress address.
      192.168.10.9 : 5 sent, **5 arrived** -- instrument alive, the path itself works.
  STEP 1 VERDICT: PASS (baseline established on both discriminators that survive scrutiny).

STEP 2 -- CONFIGURE THE SECONDARY ADDRESS.

  awplus(config)# interface vlan10
  awplus(config-if)# ip address 172.16.10.1/24 secondary
  awplus# show running-config interface vlan10
  interface vlan10
   ip address 192.168.10.1/24
   ip address 172.16.10.1/24 secondary
  awplus# show interface vlan10
    IPv4 address 172.16.10.1/24 broadcast 172.16.10.255 **secondary**
    IPv4 address 192.168.10.1/24 broadcast 192.168.10.255
  awplus# show ip route
  C       **172.16.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10**        <- a connected route appeared
  C       192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10

  🔎 **OBSERVATION O-3 (display): `show ip interface brief` shows ONLY the primary.**
    awplus# show ip interface brief
    vlan10                192.168.10.1/24    admin up        running
  The secondary is invisible there, while `show interface vlan10`, `show running-config` and
  `show ip route` all show it.  Harmless, but `show ip interface brief` cannot be used to check
  whether a secondary exists -- which is precisely the read-back the dispatch warned against
  relying on.

  2a. ARP with the feature ON -- and a result that looked like a failure until the sender was
      put in the right subnet.  --tag L2a-arp-on, sender **192.168.10.5** (the PRIMARY subnet):
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.16.10.1  tell 192.168.10.5   -> **still 0 replies**
        x4  ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43    -> 4 replies
      Taken alone that reads as "the DUT does not answer ARP for its secondary", contradicting
      the guide.  It is not: the SENDER was in the wrong subnet.

  2b. THE SAME LEG WITH THE SENDER INSIDE THE SECONDARY SUBNET.  --tag L2b-arp-on-insubnet,
      sender **172.16.10.5** / 00:11:22:33:44:05:
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.16.10.1  tell 172.16.10.5
        x4  **ARP-REPLY 172.16.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43**   -> **4 replies** [-> 00:11:22:33:44:05]
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 172.16.10.5      -> **0 replies**
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.17.10.1  tell 172.16.10.5      -> **0 replies**, control holds

      🔎 **FINDING F-4 -- the DUT answers ARP for an address on vlan10 only when the ARP
      SENDER's protocol address lies inside that address's own subnet, and this applies to the
      primary exactly as it does to the secondary.**  Measured symmetrically, which is what
      makes it a finding rather than a guess:
        sender 192.168.10.5 (primary subnet)   -> 192.168.10.1 answered, 172.16.10.1 silent
        sender 172.16.10.5  (secondary subnet) -> 172.16.10.1 answered, 192.168.10.1 silent
      So the guide's claim -- "treats secondary addresses the same as primary addresses ... such
      as responding for ARP requests for the IP address" -- **HOLDS exactly**: both are answered
      under the same rule, and both are silent under the same rule.  The practical note is that
      a tester ARPing for a secondary from a host in the primary subnet will measure silence and
      wrongly record a defect.  Both SVIs answer with the same MAC (e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43), so only
      the target address distinguishes them (platforms/x960.md §6.5).

  2c. FORWARDING INTO THE SECONDARY SUBNET, feature ON.  --tag L2c-to-secondary-on, eth4 ->
      watch eth3, sender 192.168.48.9, three destinations:
      cap on eth3 (the far side):
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> **172.16.10.5**  [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:05]
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 192.168.10.9     [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:0a]
        x1  **ARP-REQ who-has 172.16.10.5 tell 172.16.10.1**   <- the DUT ARPs AS the secondary
        x1  ARP-REPLY 172.16.10.5 is-at 00:11:22:33:44:05
      cap on eth4 (the sending side):
        x2  ICMP t3/c0 192.168.48.1 -> 192.168.48.9     <- for the unconfigured control only

      172.16.10.5  : 5 sent, **5 arrived on eth3** -- where the identical leg delivered 0 at
                     baseline.  The secondary address is genuinely serving traffic: the DUT
                     accepted a packet from another subnet, resolved the destination on vlan10
                     and delivered it.
      192.168.10.9 : 5/5 -- instrument alive.
      172.17.10.5  : 5 sent, **0 arrived** -- the negative control is dropped IN THE SAME LEG,
                     so "the DUT now forwards into 172.16.10.0/24" is not "the DUT now forwards
                     anything".
      **The DUT's own ARP request is sourced `tell 172.16.10.1`** -- the secondary address -- so
      the device uses the secondary as its interface identity on that subnet, exactly as a
      primary would.  (The guide's sentence "the only packets generated that have a secondary
      address as source address are routing updates" concerns IP source addresses; this is an
      ARP sender protocol address.  Recorded as measured, with no claim either way about the
      IP-source sentence, which this case did not test.)
  STEP 2 VERDICT: PASS.

